<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.tradingbotapp.ftmowatch</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-lc</string>
        <string>/Users/kaloyanivanov/TradingBotApp/ftmo_watch.sh</string>
    </array>

    <!-- The equity watcher, scoped to the trading session.

         Starts at 16:30 Europe/Sofia Monday to Friday and EXITS ITSELF at
         23:00 when ftmo_runner.within_trading_window() goes false. It is not
         a 24/7 daemon, and that is a deliberate reversal of how it was first
         installed on 2026-08-11.

         WHY IT IS NOT 24/7 (owner decision, same day). ftmo_watch.sh holds a
         `caffeinate -i` assertion for the lifetime of this process, so "how
         long does it run" is a battery question: an assertion held around the
         clock costs a laptop that is usually unplugged roughly 20-50Wh a
         session, for hours in which no order can be placed anyway. Session
         scope keeps the Mac awake exactly while the market is open and lets
         it sleep the other 17.5 hours and all weekend.

         Install with:
             cp com.tradingbotapp.ftmowatch.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
             launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.tradingbotapp.ftmowatch.plist
         Verify UNSANDBOXED:
             launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.tradingbotapp.ftmowatch
         A bare `launchctl list` from an agent shell reports a different domain
         and will show a loaded job as missing.

         THIS JOB CAN PLACE ORDERS — closes only, never opens. On a FLATTEN
         posture it liquidates the book without waiting for anyone. That is the
         owner's decision of 2026-08-11, taken with the alert-only alternative
         stated first, and it is the FOURTH unattended path in this project.
         To make it advisory instead, add <string>--dry-run</string> to
         ProgramArguments: it will then alert exactly as it does now and close
         nothing.

         Why it exists at all: every FTMO limit is measured on equity INCLUDING
         floating P&L, so the account can fail with no order placed. The runner
         fires 27 times inside a 6.5-hour window; this covers the other 17.5
         hours and the weekend.

         KeepAlive is SuccessfulExit=false, not true: a clean exit at the
         session close must NOT be restarted, while a crash mid-session must
         be. Plain `KeepAlive` would respawn it at 23:00:01 every night, which
         is the 24/7 daemon this design just rejected — and it would hold the
         caffeinate assertion with it.

         ThrottleInterval keeps a crash-loop from hammering the venue's
         connection limits; launchd's default of 10s is too eager for a
         process that opens a broker session on startup. A start outside the
         window costs one settings read: run_forever() refuses to connect
         before it does anything else.

         The Hour is a WILDCARD on purpose: these five entries mean "every
         hour at :30, Monday to Friday" in HOST LOCAL TIME, which is a
         deliberate superset of the Europe/Sofia session. The plist cannot
         express Sofia — it gets whatever timezone the machine happens to be
         in — so it over-fires and lets ftmo_runner.within_trading_window()
         decide, exactly as com.tradingbotapp.ftmo does. Two things fall out
         of that: a watcher that dies at 17:00 is replaced by 17:30 instead of
         being lost for the night, and taking the laptop to another timezone
         cannot silently miss the open. An out-of-window start costs one
         settings read, and a start while a watcher is already running exits
         on the lock in about a fifth of a second.

         If the Mac is asleep at 16:30, launchd runs the missed job on wake,
         so a late start is a shorter session rather than no session. If the
         LID is closed mid-session the Mac sleeps regardless of any assertion
         and the watcher stops; there is no software fix for that. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <dict>
        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>60</integer>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict><key>Weekday</key><integer>1</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Weekday</key><integer>2</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Weekday</key><integer>3</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Weekday</key><integer>4</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Weekday</key><integer>5</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
    </array>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/kaloyanivanov/TradingBotApp/ftmo_watch_launchd.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/kaloyanivanov/TradingBotApp/ftmo_watch_launchd.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
